{
  "id": "module-4",
  "courseId": "regex",
  "order": 4,
  "title": "Grouping & Concept Stacking",
  "description": "Isolate sub-expressions using (...) to apply quantifiers, establish boundaries, and build production-ready patterns.",
  "primer": {
    "syntaxToken": "(...)",
    "definition": "Parentheses group sub-patterns into distinct logical units. This allows you to apply quantifiers to multi-character blocks, enforce specific alternation boundaries, or extract structured components.",
    "goldenRule": "Quantifiers and anchors placed directly outside a group apply to the entire expression inside the parentheses.",
    "mentalModel": "Think of a group as a container that bundles several characters into one token. Once wrapped in (...), that whole block behaves like a single character for the purposes of quantifiers, alternation, and anchoring — the same way parentheses in arithmetic change what an operator applies to.",
    "points": [
      "Parentheses create a capturing group by default: the text matched inside is remembered and can be extracted or referenced, not just grouped for repetition.",
      "A quantifier placed immediately after a closing parenthesis repeats the entire grouped sub-pattern, not just the character before the parenthesis.",
      "Alternation with | is scoped to the nearest enclosing group — (cat|dog) only chooses between 'cat' and 'dog', while an ungrouped cat|dog can split a larger surrounding pattern in half.",
      "Groups are numbered left-to-right by their opening parenthesis, starting at 1, which matters once you have more than one group in a pattern."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
      "Assuming a quantifier outside a group only repeats the last character inside it — (\\d{2}){2} matches four digits total (two groups of two), not two digits repeated twice.",
      "Forgetting to group alternation inside a larger pattern: ^GET|POST$ anchors 'GET' only at the start and 'POST' only at the end, which is not the same as ^(GET|POST)$ anchoring both options on both ends.",
      "Treating every () as free — each capturing group adds an entry to the match result, so stacking groups you don't need to reference still changes what the match object returns."
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "pattern": "(ab)+",
        "match": "abab",
        "note": "The + quantifier repeats the entire grouped unit 'ab', not just the letter 'b'."
      },
      {
        "pattern": "(cat|dog)",
        "match": "dog",
        "note": "Alternation inside parentheses is scoped to just the two listed options."
      },
      {
        "pattern": "^(\\d{3})-(\\d{4})$",
        "match": "555-1234",
        "note": "Two independent groups can sit side by side to capture separate structured components of one pattern."
      }
    ]
  },
  "cards": [
